        'The classic method calculates two snapshots: the winter evening for offtake and the sunny afternoon for feed-in, with fixed simultaneity. That is safe, but often too cautious.\n\nA time-series load flow runs the load flow for all 8,760 hours or 35,040 quarter-hours of a year, with a profile per connection type. Per asset you then get the exceedance duration and the energy above the limit, explained with the load duration curve in The electricity grid.',
        'The profiles come from summed smart-meter data, from standard profiles per customer type and from models: heat pumps that follow the outdoor temperature, car charging sessions.\n\nA probabilistic load flow goes further: it draws hundreds of times at random who has a heat pump or car and when it charges, and calculates each draw. You get a distribution of the peak. Planners then pick a percentile, for example the 95th: the peak that is not exceeded in 95% of the draws.',
        'Hosting capacity is how much extra feed-in or offtake can be added at a point before a limit is hit: rating, voltage band or protection. At the end of a long feeder it is usually lowest, because there the voltage leaves the band first during feed-in.\n\nThe public capacity map is a coarse version per station; hosting capacity per feeder or per cable is finer.',
        'What it delivers: often a limit turns out to be hit only a few hours a year, and then flexibility is cheaper than reinforcement (see the flexibility topic). What it needs: a correct network model in the GIS and good measurement data (see Grid operations and assets).\n\nAnd a reference year: the weather year you calculate with. A mild year underestimates heat pumps; many grid operators choose a cold year, for example a winter that occurs once in ten years.',
        'Experts argue about how much risk the 95th percentile takes: in one case in twenty the real peak is above it, and who carries that? Supporters say worst-case planning digs up streets years too early; opponents point to the first really cold week.\n\nPitfall: a fine model on poor network data. A sharp question: “Which percentile, which reference year, and what happens in the worst hour?”',
